  "facts": [
    "Long Gully Bridge opened in January 1892 as a 152‑metre steel suspension marvel, charging threepence for adults and one penny for children, drawing tourists to its sandstone‑turreted towers.",
    "In 1937‑39 the bridge was transformed into a 105‑metre reinforced‑concrete arch, preserving the iconic sandstone towers while replacing corroded cables, a pioneering heritage‑sensitive engineering solution.",
    "After reopening in late 1939, the bridge received Sydney electricity floodlights in 1992 and safety fencing in 2010, keeping its historic silhouette lit and secure for modern commuters."
